  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nCurrently, high amplitude upper level ridge extends from the\nSouthern Rockies north into south central Canada, with a deep\nupper low/long wave trough over the eastern CONUS. This results\nin continued northerly flow aloft over the Northern Plains, with\nsfc high pressure over the eastern Dakotas and lee side \ntroughing to our west. South/southeasterly flow, with breezy \nconditions at times west this afternoon. Stratus continues to \nerode, with a south to north orientated stratus deck from south \ncentral South Dakota north-northwest through central and \nwestern North Dakota, basically along Highway 83 to Highway 85. \nSome erosion on the western and eastern fringes of this layer, \nbut with the sun soon setting unsure how much more will \ndissipate. Same goes for the lingering stratus east, as it \neroded nicely for awhile, but now appears to linger more. RRFS \nand RAP both agree with low clouds again tonight where the \ncurrent stratus is at, with possible redevelopment elsewhere. \nAlso, both RRFS and RAP show fog developing once again on the \nwestern edge of the stratus over far western ND, and have this \nin the forecast and a mention in the HWO. \n\nBlocking pattern continues into the weekend, with sharp ridging\nremaining quasi-stationary/nudging east into the Great Plains,\nand long wave troughing east and developing troughing west.\nResultant weather will be dry, with temperatures slowly warming\nFriday into the weekend as the ridge approaches our region. \nThis warming trend continues into early next week, when we will\nonce again see well above normal high temperatures in the 50s \nto lower 60s. South/southeasterly winds will also increase\nFriday into Saturday with a tightening pressure gradient, breezy\nwest tomorrow , then breezy area wide for Saturday. Pressure\ngradient relaxes and winds won't be as breezy for Sunday as the\naforementioned ridge continue to slowly push east/collapses \nover the Dakotas. \n\nUpper ridging will continue to develop east into the eastern \nCONUS early next week, setting the stage for a more active and \ncooler weather pattern developing across the Rockies into the \nNorthern Plains towards the middle of next week. Ensembles and \nNBM all support this.",
